          body: `Cold is the most widely recommended comfort for sore gums, but there is a clear line at frozen solid. The NHS says some teething rings can be chilled in the fridge, following the packaging, but never put a teething ring in the freezer, because it could damage your baby's gums if it gets frozen. The FDA says to make sure the ring is not frozen and warns that a hard object can hurt the gums. The AAP says not to let your baby chew directly on anything frozen solid.\n\nThe official pages are not perfectly uniform here. The AAP suggests a damp washcloth, twisted up and cooled, and cold water in a cup for babies over 6 months. The German health portal kindergesundheit-info.de says chilled teething rings should not be ice cold. The Turkish Ministry of Health page lists frozen foods such as banana or carrot, and ice wrapped in a cloth, for pressure relief, whereas the NHS and AAP advise against anything frozen solid. A sensible reading is to follow the stricter rule: cool, not frozen solid, and always with an adult watching.`,
